†9 Functional Notations L94:     1. cite79†9.1 Numeric Functions L95:     2. cite80†9.2 Serialization of Functional Notations L96:   10. cite81†10 Mathematical Expressions L97:     1. cite82†10.1 Basic Arithmetic: calc() L98:     2. cite83†10.2 Comparison Functions: min(), max(), and clamp() L99:     3. cite84†10.3 Stepped Value Functions: round(), mod(), and rem() L100:       1. cite85†10.3.1 Argument Ranges L101:     4. cite86†10.4 Trigonometric Functions: sin(), cos(), tan(), asin(), acos(), atan(), and atan2() L102:       1. cite87†10.4.1 Argument Ranges L103:     5. cite88†10.5 Exponential Functions: pow(), sqrt(), hypot(), log(), exp() L104:       1. cite89†10.5.1 Argument Ranges L105:     6. cite90†10.6 Sign-Related Functions: abs(), sign() L106:     7. cite91†10.7 Numeric Keywords L107:       1. cite92†10.7.1 Numeric Constants: e, pi L108:       2. cite93†10.7.2 Degenerate Numeric Constants: infinity, -infinity, NaN L109:       3. cite94†10.7.3 Numeric Variables L110:     8. cite95†10.8 Syntax L111:     9. cite96†10.9 Type Checking L112:       1. cite97†10.9.1 Calculation Contexts L113:       2. cite98†10.9.2 Infinities, NaN, and Signed Zero L114:     10. cite99†10.10 Internal Representation L115:       1. cite100†10.10.1 Simplification L116:     11. cite101†10.11 Computed Value L117:     12. cite102†10.12 Range Checking L118:     13. cite103†10.13 Serialization L119:     14. cite104†10.14 Combination of Math Functions L120:   11. cite105†Appendix A: Coordinating List-Valued Properties L121:   12. cite106†Appendix B: IANA Considerations L122:     1. cite107†Registration for the `about:invalid` URL scheme L123:   13. cite108†Appendix C: Quirky Lengths L124:   14. cite109†Acknowledgments L125:   15. cite110†Changes L126:     1. cite111†Recent Changes L127:     2. cite112†Additions Since Level 3 L128:   16. cite113†Security Considerations L129:   17. cite114†Privacy Considerations L130:   18. cite115†Conformance L131:     1. cite116†Document conventions L132:     2. cite117†Conformance classes L133:     3. cite118†Partial implementations L134:       1. cite119†Implementations of Unstable and Proprietary Features L135:     4. cite120†Non-experimental implementations L136:   19. cite121†Index L137:     1. cite122†Terms defined by this specification L138:     2. cite123†Terms defined by reference L139:   20. cite124†References L140:     1. cite125†Normative References L141:     2. cite126†Non-Normative References L142:   21. cite4†Issues Index L143: ## 1. Introduction\nL144: \nL145: The value definition field of each CSS property can contain keywords, data types (which appear between < and >), and information on how they can be combined. Generic data types (cite127†<length> being the most widely used) that can be used by many properties are described in this specification, while more specific data types (e.g., <spacing-limit>) are described in the corresponding modules.\nL146: ### 1.1. Module Interactions\nL147: \nL148: This module replaces and extends the data type definitions in cite128†[CSS2] sections cite129†1.4.2.1†www.w3.org , cite130†4.3†www.w3.org , and cite131†A.2†www.w3.org .\nL149: \nL150: ## 2. Value Definition Syntax\nL151: \nL152: The value definition syntax described here is used to define the set of valid values for CSS properties (and the valid syntax of many other parts of CSS). A value so described can have one or more components.\nL153: ### 2.1. Component Value Types\nL154: \nL155: Component value types are designated in several ways:\nL156: \nL157:   1. cite33†Keyword values (such as `auto`, cite132†disc , etc.) and at-keywords representing the start of an cite133†at-rule , which appear literally, without quotes (e.g. `auto` or `@media`).\nL158: Note: It is possible, with cite134†escaping , to construct a cite135†CSS identifier whose value ends with `(` or starts with `@`. Such a token is an cite136†<ident-token> (i.e. a cite137†keyword ), not a cite138†<function-token> or an cite139†<at-keyword-token> .\nL159: \nL160:   2. Basic data types, which appear between `<` and `>` (e.g., cite127†<length> , cite140†<percentage> , etc.). For cite141†numeric data types , this type notation can annotate any range restrictions using the cite45†bracketed range notation described below.\nL161:   3. Property value ranges, which represent the same pattern of values as a property bearing the same name. These are written as the property name, surrounded by single quotes, between `<` and `>`, e.g., cite142†<'border-width'> , cite143†<'background-attachment'> , etc.\nL162: These types do not include cite34†CSS-wide keywords such as cite144†inherit . Additionally, if the property’s value grammar is a cite145†comma-separated repetition , the corresponding type does not include the top-level comma-separated list multiplier. (E.g. if a property named `pairing` is defined as `[ cite146†<custom-ident> cite147†<integer> ? ]#`, then `<'pairing'>` is equivalent to `[ cite146†<custom-ident> cite147†<integer> ? ]`, not `[ cite146†<custom-ident> cite147†<integer> ? ]#`.)\nL163: \nL164: Why remove the multiplier?\nL165: The top-level multiplier is ripped out of these value types because top-level comma-separated repetitions are mostly used for cite148†coordinating list properties , and when a shorthand combines several such properties, it needs the unmultiplied grammar so it can construct its own comma-separated repetition.\nL166: \nL167: Without this special treatment, every such longhand would have to be defined with an ad-hoc production just for the inner value, which makes the grammars harder to understand overall.\nL168:   4. Functional notations and their arguments. These may be written literally as defined in cite27†§ 2.6 Functional Notation Definitions , or referenced by a non-terminal using the function’s name, followed by an empty parentheses pair, between `<` and `>`, e.g. cite149†<calc()> , and references the correspondingly-named cite150†functional notation .\nL169:   5. Other non-terminals. These are written as the name of the non-terminal between `<` and `>`, as in <spacing-limit>. Notice the distinction between cite151†<border-width> and cite142†<'border-width'> : the latter represents the grammar of the border-width property, the former requires an explicit expansion elsewhere. The definition of a non-terminal is typically located near its first appearance in the specification.\nL170:   6. Delimiters, which represent their corresponding tokens. Slashes (`/`), cite152†commas (`,`), colons (`:`), semicolons (`;`), parentheses (`(` and `)`), and braces (`{` and `}`) are written literally. Other delimiters must be written enclosed in single quotes (such as `'+'`).\nL171: Commas specified in the grammar are implicitly omissible in some circumstances, when used to separate optional terms in the grammar. Within a top-level list in a property or other CSS value, or a function’s argument list, a comma specified in the grammar must be omitted if:\nL172:   * all items preceding the comma have been omitted\nL173:   * all items following the comma have been omitted\nL174:   * multiple commas would be adjacent (ignoring cite153†white space†www.w3.org /comments), due to the items between the commas being omitted.\nL175: \nL176: For example, if a function can accept three arguments in order, but all of them are optional, the grammar can be written like:\nL177: \nL178:     example( firstcite154†? cite152†, secondcite154†? cite152†, thirdcite154†? )\nL179: Given this grammar, writing example(first, second, third) is valid, as is example(first, second) or example(first, third) or example(second). However, example(first, , third) is invalid, as one of those commas are no longer separating two options; similarly, example(,second) and example(first,) are invalid. example(first second) is also invalid, as commas are still required to actually separate the options.\nL180: If commas were not implicitly omittable, the grammar would have to be much more complicated to properly express the ways that the arguments can be omitted, greatly obscuring the simplicity of the feature.\nL181: All CSS properties also accept the cite34†CSS-wide keyword values as the sole component of their property value. For readability these are not listed explicitly in the property value syntax definitions. For example, the full value definition of cite155†border-color under cite156†CSS Cascading and Inheritance Level 3 is `<color>{1,4} | inherit | initial | unset` (even though it is listed as `<color>{1,4}`).\nL182: Note: This implies that, in general, combining these keywords with other component values in the same declaration results in an invalid declaration. For example, cite157†background: url(corner.png) no-repeat, inherit; is invalid.\nL183: ### 2.2. Component Value Combinators\nL184: \nL185: Component values can be arranged into property values as follows:\nL186: \nL187:   * Juxtaposing components means that all of them must occur, in the given order.\nL188:   * A double ampersand (&&) separates two or more components, all of which must occur, in any order.\nL189:   * A double bar (||) separates two or more options: one or more of them must occur, in any order.\nL190:   * A bar (|) separates two or more alternatives: exactly one of them must occur.\nL191:   * Brackets ([ ]) are for grouping.\nL192: Juxtaposition is stronger than the double ampersand, the double ampersand is stronger than the double bar, and the double bar is stronger than the bar. Thus, the following lines are equivalent:\nL193: \nL194:       a b   |   c ||   d &&   e f\nL195:     [ a b ] | [ c || [ d && [ e f ]]]\nL196: \nL197: \nL198: For reorderable combinators (||, &&), ordering of the grammar does not matter: components in the same grouping may be interleaved in any order. Thus, the following lines are equivalent:\nL199: \nL200:     a || b || c\nL201:     b || a || c\nL202: Note: Combinators are not associative, so grouping is significant. For example, a || b || c and a || [ b || c ] are distinct grammars: the first allows a value like b a c, but the second does not.\nL203: ### 2.3. Component Value Multipliers\nL204: \nL205: Every type, keyword, or bracketed group may be followed by one of the following modifiers:\nL206:   * An asterisk (*) indicates that the preceding type, word, or group occurs zero or more times.\nL207:   * A plus (+) indicates that the preceding type, word, or group occurs one or more times.\nL208:   * A question mark (?) indicates that the preceding type, word, or group is optional (occurs zero or one times).\nL209:   * A single number in curly braces ({`A`}) indicates that the preceding type, word, or group occurs `A` times.\nL210:   * A comma-separated pair of numbers in curly braces ({`A`,`B`}) indicates that the preceding type, word, or group occurs at least `A` and at most `B` times. The `B` may be omitted ({`A`,}) to indicate that there must be at least `A` repetitions, with no upper bound on the number of repetitions.\nL211:   * A hash mark (#) indicates that the preceding type, word, or group occurs one or more times, separated by comma tokens (which may optionally be surrounded by cite153†white space†www.w3.org and/or comments). It may optionally be followed by the curly brace forms, above, to indicate precisely how many times the repetition occurs, like <length>#{1,4}.\nL212:   * An exclamation point (!) after a group indicates that the group is required and must produce at least one value; even if the grammar of the items within the group would otherwise allow the entire contents to be omitted, at least one component value must not be omitted.\nL213: The + and # multipliers may be stacked as +#; similarly, the # and ? multipliers, {A} and ? multipliers, and {A,B} and ? multipliers may be stacked as #?, {A}?, and {A,B}?, respectively. These stacks each represent the later multiplier applied to the result of the earlier multiplier. (These same stacks can be represented using grouping, but in complex grammars this can push the number of brackets beyond readability.)\nL214: For repeated component values (indicated by *, +, or #), cite158†UAs must support at least 20 repetitions of the component. If a property value contains more than the supported number of repetitions, the declaration must be ignored as if it were invalid.\nL215: ### 2.4. Combinator and Multiplier Patterns\nL216: \nL217: There are a small set of common ways to combine multiple independent cite159†component values in particular numbers and orders. In particular, it’s common to want to express that, from a set of component value, the author must select zero or more, one or more, or all of them, and in either the order specified in the grammar or in any order.\nL218: \nL219: All of these can be easily expressed using simple patterns of cite23†combinators and cite24†multipliers :\nL220:  | in order  | any order\nL221: --- | --- | ---\nL222: zero or more  | `A? B? C?`  | `A? || B? || C?`\nL223: one or more  | `[ A? B? C? ]!`  | `A || B || C`\nL224: all  | `A B C `  | `A && B && C`\nL225: \nL226: Note that all of the \"any order\" possibilities are expressed using combinators, while the \"in order\" possibilities are all variants on juxtaposition.\nL227: ### 2.5. Component Values and White Space\nL228: \nL229: Unless otherwise specified, cite153†white space†www.w3.org and/or comments may appear before, after, and/or between components combined using the above cite23†combinators and cite24†multipliers .\nL230: Note: In many cases, spaces will in fact be required between components in order to distinguish them from each other. For example, the value 1em2em would be parsed as a single cite160†<dimension-token> with the number 1 and the identifier em2em, which is an invalid unit. In this case, a space would be required before the 2 to get this parsed as the two lengths 1em and 2em.\nL231: ### 2.6. Functional Notation Definitions\nL232: \nL233: The syntax of a cite150†functional notation is defined as a sequence of:\nL234: \nL235:   1. The function’s name written as an identifier followed by an open parenthesis (such as example(), or the cite138†<function-token> production to indicate a function with an arbitrary name.\nL236: \nL237:   2. The function’s arguments, if any, expressed using the cite161†value definition syntax .\nL238: \nL239:   3. A literal closing parenthesis.\nL240: The function’s arguments are considered implicitly grouped, as if surrounded by brackets ([ ... ]).\nL241: \nL242: For example, a grammar like:\nL243: \nL244:     example( <length> cite152†, <length> )\nL245: \nL246: \nL247: will match a function whose name is \"example\" and whose arguments match \"cite127†<length> , <length>\".\nL248: For example, the Selectors grammar defines pseudo-classes generically, allowing any possibly function name after the initial colon:\nL249: \nL250:     <pseudo-class-selector> = : <ident-token> cite162†| : <function-token> <any-value> )\nL251: \nL252: \nL253: This represents any function name, with cite163†<any-value> as the function arguments.\nL254: Since the cite150†functional notation implicitly groups its contents, the effect of any combinator inside it is scoped to the function’s argument. For example, the functional notation syntax definition example( foo | bar ) is equivalent to example( [ foo | bar ] ).\nL255: ### 2.7. Property Value Examples\nL256: \nL257: Below are some examples of properties with their corresponding value definition fields\nL258: Property  | Value definition field  | Example value\nL259: --- | --- | ---\nL260: cite164†orphans | <integer>  | 3\nL261: cite165†text-align | left | right | center | justify  | cite166†center L262: cite167†padding-top | <length> | <percentage>  | 5%\nL263: cite168†outline-color | <color> | invert  | #fefefe\nL264: cite169†text-decoration | none | underline || overline || line-through || blink  | overline underline\nL265: cite170†font-family | [ <font-family-name> | <generic-font-family> ]#  | \"Gill Sans\", Futura, sans-serif\nL266: cite142†border-width | [ <length> | thick | medium | thin ]{1,4}  | 2px medium 4px\nL267: cite171†box-shadow | [ inset? && <length>{2,4} && <color>? ]# | none  | 3px 3px rgba(50%, 50%, 50%, 50%), lemonchiffon 0 0 4px inset\nL268: ### 2.8. Non-Terminal Definitions and Grammar Production Blocks\nL269: \nL270: The precise grammar of non-terminals, like cite172†<position> or cite149†<calc()> , is often specified in a CSS grammar production block. These are conventionally represented in a preformatted block of definitions like this:\nL271: \nL272: The <foo> syntax is defined as follows:\nL273: \nL274:     <foo> = keyword cite162†| <bar> cite162†| L275:             some-really-long-pattern-of-stuff\nL276:     <bar> = <length>\nL277: Each definition starts on its own line, and consists of the non-terminal to be defined, followed by an `=`, followed by the fragment of cite161†value definition syntax to which it expands. A definition can stretch across multiple lines, and terminates before the next line that starts a new grammar production or at the end of the grammar production block (whichever comes first).\nL278: In the above example, the <foo> definition covers two lines. The third line starts a new definition for <bar>. (A naked `=` is never valid in cite161†value definition syntax , so it’s unambiguous when a new line starts a fresh definition.)\nL279: ## 3. Combining Values: Interpolation, Addition, and Accumulation\nL280: Some procedures, for example cite173†transitions†www.w3.org and cite174†animations†www.w3.org , combine two CSS property values. The following combining operations—​on the two cite175†computed values `V_{A}` and `V_{B}` yielding the computed value `V_{result}`—​are defined. For operations that are not commutative (for example, matrix multiplication, or accumulation of mismatched transform lists) `V_{A}` represents the first term of the operation and `V_{B}` represents the second.\nL281: interpolation\nL282:     Given two property values `V_{A}` and `V_{B}`, produces an intermediate value `V_{result}` at a distance of `p` along the interval between `V_{A}` and `V_{B}` such that `p` = 0 produces `V_{A}` and `p` = 1 produces `V`_{B}.\nL283: \nL284: The range of `p` is (−∞, ∞) due to the effect of cite176†timing functions . As a result, this procedure must also define extrapolation behavior for `p` outside [0, 1].\nL285: addition\nL286:     Given two property values `V_{A}` and `V_{B}`, returns the sum of the two properties, `V`_{result}.\nL287: \nL288: Note: While cite177†addition can often be expressed in terms of the same weighted sum function used to define cite178†interpolation , this is not always the case. For example, interpolation of transform matrices involves decomposing and interpolating the matrix components whilst addition relies on matrix multiplication.\nL289: If a value type does not define a specific procedure for cite177†addition or is defined as not additive, its addition operation is simply `V_{result}` = `V_{B}`.\nL290: \nL291: accumulation\nL292:     Given two property values `V_{A}` and `V_{B}`, returns the result, `V_{result}`, of combining the two operands such that `V_{B}` is treated as a delta from `V_{A}`.\nL293: \nL294: Note: For many types of animation such as numbers or lengths, cite179†accumulation is defined to be identical to cite177†addition .\nL295: A common case where the definitions differ is for list-based types where cite177†addition may be defined as appending to a list whilst cite179†accumulation may be defined as component-based addition. For example, the filter list values blur(2) and blur(3), when added together would produce blur(2) blur(3), but when accumulated would produce blur(5).\nL296: \nL297: If a value type does not define a specific procedure for cite179†accumulation , its accumulation operation is identical to cite177†addition .\nL298: These operations are only defined on cite175†computed values . (As a result, it is not necessary to define, for example, how to add a cite127†<length> value of 15pt with 5em since such values will be resolved to their cite180†canonical unit before being passed to any of the above procedures.)\nL299: ### 3.1. Range Checking\nL300: Interpolation can result in a value outside the valid range for a property, even if all of the inputs to interpolation are valid; this especially happens when `p` is outside the [0, 1] range, but some cite176†easing functions can cause this to occur even within that range. If the final result after interpolation, addition, and accumulation is out-of-range for the target context the value is being used in, it does not cause the declaration to be invalid.\nL301: Instead, the value must be clamped to the range allowed in the target context, exactly the same as cite181†math functions (see cite102†§ 10.12 Range Checking ).\nL302: Note: Even if interpolation results in an out-of-range value, addition/accumulation might \"correct\" the result and bring it back into range. Thus, clamping is only applied to the final result of applying all interpolation-related operations.\nL303: ## 4. Textual Data Types\nL304: The textual data types include various keywords and identifiers as well as strings (cite182†<string> ) and URLs (cite183†<url> ). Aside from the casing of cite33†pre-defined keywords or as explicitly defined for a given property, no normalization is performed, not even Unicode normalization: the cite184†specified and cite175†computed value of a property are exactly the provided Unicode values after parsing (which includes character set conversion and cite185†escaping ). cite186†[UNICODE] cite187†[CSS-SYNTAX-3] L305: cite188†Strings†infra.spec.whatwg.org are quoted sequences of characters reprenting arbitrary textual data. See cite37†§ 4.4 Quoted Strings: the <string> type for details.\nL306: CSS identifiers, generically denoted by <ident>, represent names, and are written as an unquoted sequence of (potentially escaped) characters corresponding to the cite136†<ident-token> grammar. cite187†[CSS-SYNTAX-3] Identifiers cannot be quoted; otherwise they would be interpreted as strings. CSS properties accept two classes of cite135†identifiers : cite33†pre-defined keywords and cite35†author-defined identifiers .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Properties and Values API Level 1 (https://drafts.css-houdini.org/css-properties-values-api-1/)\nciteturn1278view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.css-houdini.org/css-properties-values-api-1/#computed-value-time-behavior\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1339\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Values and Units Module Level 5 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/)\nciteturn1278view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/#substitution-in-properties\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 2842\n"
